ually has some some  real-world applications but so did you  know just to demonstrate it we said well  look if we took the London Underground  that's just the specific graph so we  haven't trained this on Transport graphs  on you know it hasn't seen the London  Underground in train hasn't seen  anything that corresponds at all to  transport or anything like that it's  just completely random graphs but if  it's really learned how to parse graphs  how to  manipulate them how to perform these  kinds of tasks and essentially parsing a  graph means getting the whole graph  structure in some completely random  order putting it all in memory and then  being able to operate on that memory  such that you can use the the structure  of the graph if that makes sense so it's  kind of like in some sense you're  sequencing the graph but the sequence is  random and now it's just a whole bunch  of stuff written into memory that you  then have to traverse and find your way  around in order to answer the problems  anyway in the case of London Underground  the question becomes something like  start at Oxford Circus for the central  line then circle line circle line circle  and so on and make all these steps and  where do you end up and you know it  could answer those ones no problem a  slightly more challenging kind of well  actually a lot more challenging task is  find the shortest path between two  points in the graph so really what you  want to do is to you know solve solve a  shortest path algorithm which you know  we know we can do for arbitrary graphs  in practice it didn't solve it didn't  solve the algorithm right it didn't find  a sort of optimal path finding solution  it found something much more heuristic  that in a way is more like what a person  would do like when we we analyze how the  network was behaving it would kind of  look it's so it's being given this you  know the question the form of the  question is here's the start station  here's the end station now give me the  route and it would start looking it  would start at you know either end of  the sequence and look around a little  bit look a few steps around and attempt  to kind of find a way to join them up  which I mean is not as I say it's not  not unlike what a person might do  looking at the same problem but it  certainly wasn't you know optimal in any  sense but it could do up to maybe length  length v or length 6  you know paths it did a pretty good job  and it could find these you know these  these routes on the London Underground  another set of problems that we wanted  to look at was inference and again this  is something that has real-world  implications for for for question  answering for knowledge graph and so  forth and so we set up the the inference  problems again we trained it all on you  know on random graphs  form of the problem would be that you're  given a you're given an implicit link  which is not stated in the graph itself  in this case maternal great-uncle has a  specific meaning which is a essentially  a traversal right it's a chain of  relationships in the graph your your  fathers and mothers brothers whatever I  can't remember but but it's never  explicitly represented in the graph it  just has to work out what that means and  then so in this and and I should say  again during training there's no notion  of you know great uncles or  grandmother's or anything it's given  completely random labels with completely  random links that have particular  meanings but then you know to illustrate  it we took this family tree example that  makes sense to us as people and we could  ask it who is Freya's maternal  great-uncle and it can answer by  following this inferred chain of  relations and this slide shows so you  know the the analysis gets a little bit  more complicated this complicated slide  shows how it does these these things how  it reads these graphs again it uses a  combination of all of the these  [Music]  attention you know memory mechanisms  that 